module product.fabric.memory_grant

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.measure { ByteSize, FiniteByteSize, byte_size_count, finite_byte_size_count }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }
import product.fabric.envelope { MemoryRequirement }
import product.fabric.identity { FabricIdentity, WorkKey, ExecutionAttemptKey }

// A REQUIREMENT IS NOT A GRANT, AND THIS MODULE EXISTS BECAUSE THE FABRIC HAD ONLY THE FIRST WORD.
//
// product.fabric.envelope MemoryRequirement.min_bytes says "the Work needs AT LEAST N bytes". It is
// a fact about the WORK, censored from below, stated by whoever authored the Work: it bounds nothing
// above, it authorizes nothing, and it is not a quantity a realization may hand to a VMM as a size.
// A GRANT says "THIS ATTEMPT IS AUTHORIZED TO CONSUME G BYTES" -- a fact about ONE ATTEMPT, decided
// by whoever owns the capacity, under a named policy, and a CEILING the realization provisions
// against. They are different facts even when a policy chooses G = N, which is exactly why the
// conflation is cheap to write and expensive to find: the two numbers agree at the one input where
// a reader would check, and disagree on who may state them, what revises them, and what a consumer
// may conclude. The remedies differ too: a requirement too large is a Work to reduce, a grant too
// small is a cell row to raise.
//
// gunbc.runner_microvm runner_microvm_shape_after_cpu_admitted already READS the requirement, and
// reads it correctly -- as a FIT GATE. Every Work it admits then receives the SAME guest memory,
// the whole cell remainder, because there is no carrier in which "what this attempt may consume"
// could be said at all. This is that carrier, and nothing in this change hands it to a realization.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A FIELD ON ExecutionGrant, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A SECOND GRANT AUTHORITY. An
// ExecutionGrant authorizes an attempt TO EXECUTE against an offer and a quote; its evidence is
// admission, budget and lease. A memory grant quantifies ONE RESOURCE AXIS of that authorization,
// is produced by a per-cell sizing policy rather than by the admission join, and has its own
// refusal vocabulary. The relation is declared rather than left to a reader: a memory grant NAMES
// the Work and the Attempt an ExecutionGrant authorizes, so the join is by identity and neither
// carrier is the other's field.
//
// THE CARRIER IS PROVIDER-NEUTRAL, LIKE EVERY OTHER product.fabric CARRIER: it names its subject
// through FabricIdentity<P, _> and its policy through a DeclarationRef. It knows no cell, no VMM, no
// fleet and no number. The policy that chooses G lives in a gunbc.fabric_* authority
// (gunbc.fabric_memory_grant_policy), which is where an operator decision belongs.

// THE COMPATIBILITY PERMIT IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES AN UNSTATED AXIS GRANTABLE, and it is a
// named, reasoned record rather than a Bool for the reason every other authorization in this corpus
// is: a Bool records that someone said yes and loses who and why, and a permit that cannot be named
// cannot be revoked, counted, or given a runway. It carries the default it authorizes, so the
// number a defaulted grant received is the permit's own and not one the issuer chose beside it.
type MemoryCompatibilityPermit {
  default_grant: ByteSize
  permitted_by: DeclarationRef
  reason: NonEmptyStr
}

// PROVENANCE CARRIES THE REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN SITTING BESIDE IT, so the two cannot disagree.
//
// The obvious shape is a `required_minimum: MemoryRequirement?` field beside a provenance tag. That
// shape makes two contradictory states writable -- an absent minimum tagged as derived from a
// stated requirement, and a present minimum tagged as a compatibility default -- and then owes a
// validator to reject them. DESIGN 5 prefers the construction: the stated arm HOLDS the requirement
// it was derived from, the permitted arm holds the permit it was issued under, and neither state
// can be written. It is also what makes the rendering law at the foot of this module structural: a
// defaulted grant has NO minimum to report, so no fold over this type can render one as
// requirement-checked, because there is nothing there to render.
//
// AND IT IS WHAT MAKES "AN UNSTATED AXIS WITHOUT A PERMIT" UNWRITABLE HERE RATHER THAN CHECKED. A
// grant needs a provenance; a provenance needs a requirement or a permit; so a grant over an axis
// nobody stated, issued under no permit, has no constructor. The policy that faces an absent axis
// with no permit REFUSES on its own typed arm (gunbc.fabric_memory_grant_policy
// GrantRefusedUnstatedAxisWithoutPermit), and this carrier has no check to restate.
type MemoryGrantProvenance
  = GrantedAgainstStatedRequirement { required: MemoryRequirement }
  | GrantedUnderCompatibilityPermit { permit: MemoryCompatibilityPermit }

// THE GRANT. sole_constructor, so the only route to one is issue_memory_grant below, which cannot
// be reached without the things a grant must not be assembled without: the subject it authorizes,
// the provenance of its number, and the two laws the fold decides.
//
// THE AUTHORITY IS A TYPE PARAMETER, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES "ONE PRODUCER" A CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN
// A SENTENCE. sole_constructor confines construction to THIS module, and this module is
// provider-neutral vocabulary -- it must not import a fleet's sizing policy, or the neutral layer
// would depend on our operator rows. So the producer monopoly is expressed the other way round: A
// is supplied by the caller, the policy that owns the numbers declares its own sole_constructor
// authority token, and a MemoryGrant<P, ThatAuthority> is therefore unconstructible outside the
// policy's module because nothing else can mint the token to pass in. A consumer that demands
// grants of a particular authority type has a structural guarantee about who sized them, not a
// convention. (An admit_callers roster on a mint is NOT the equivalent: it compares caller
// identity on the call edge and constrains nothing about the value's egress.)
//
// THE CEILING, STATED: this establishes WHO minted the grant, that its number is not below a stated
// minimum, and that it is not above the ceiling the issuer declared. It does not establish that the
// number is WISE or that anything enforced it at runtime. Those are the policy's and the
// realization's obligations respectively.
type MemoryGrant<P, A> sole_constructor {
  work: FabricIdentity<P, WorkKey>
  attempt: FabricIdentity<P, ExecutionAttemptKey>
  granted: ByteSize
  provenance: MemoryGrantProvenance
  policy: DeclarationRef
  authority: A
}

fn memory_grant_bytes<P, A>(g: MemoryGrant<P, A>) -> ByteSize {
  g.granted
}

// THE OPTIONAL MINIMUM, DERIVED RATHER THAN STORED. A grant issued under a compatibility permit has
// no required minimum -- not a zero one, and not an unknown one: the Work stated none.
fn memory_grant_required_minimum<P, A>(g: MemoryGrant<P, A>) -> MemoryRequirement? {
  match g.provenance {
    GrantedAgainstStatedRequirement { required: r } => Present { value: r }
    GrantedUnderCompatibilityPermit { permit: _ } => none
  }
}

// THE TWO LAWS THIS CARRIER OWNS, AND NEITHER ONE DEGRADES.
//
// GrantBelowStatedMinimum is the law the whole module exists for: a policy may grant MORE than the
// Work asked for and may never grant less. A grant derived from a stated requirement that is below
// that requirement is not a grant, it is a smaller product delivered under the name of the one that
// was asked for. Raising the number to the minimum here would be the absorbing fallback DESIGN 5
// forbids wearing a helpful face: the policy would be silently overridden and nothing would count
// it.
//
// GrantAboveCeiling REFUSES AND MUST NEVER CLAMP. Clamping is the absorbing fallback in its purest
// form: the policy sized G, the cell cannot hold G, and a clamp silently provisions something
// smaller and reports success. The attempt then dies inside the guest, at a layer that cannot
// attribute the death to this decision, and the signal that the cell row is too small for the
// demand is destroyed at the one place it existed. The remedy differs by case -- raise the cell
// row, lower the allowance, or reduce the demand -- and a clamp picks one silently. So the refusal
// carries BOTH numbers and the line stops. The ceiling is a FiniteByteSize so a zero ceiling is
// unwritable: a zero ceiling is not a small ceiling, it is the absence of one.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the representability of the number itself. A grant the policy could
// not COMPUTE is refused by the policy before this fold is reached (gunbc.fabric_memory_grant_policy
// GrantRefusedUnrepresentable), because this fold cannot say "above the ceiling" about a number that
// does not exist. Both laws below compare figures that were established to be figures.
type MemoryGrantRefusal
  = GrantBelowStatedMinimum { required: ByteSize, granted: ByteSize }
  | GrantAboveCeiling { granted: ByteSize, ceiling: FiniteByteSize }

type MemoryGrantIssue<P, A>
  = MemoryGranted { grant: MemoryGrant<P, A> }
  | MemoryGrantRefused { refusal: MemoryGrantRefusal }

fn memory_grant_refusal_text(r: MemoryGrantRefusal) -> String {
  match r {
    GrantBelowStatedMinimum { required: q, granted: g } =>
      join(["the policy granted ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: g)), " bytes for a Work that states a minimum of ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: q)), " bytes; a grant below the stated minimum authorizes an attempt the Work already said cannot run"], "")
    GrantAboveCeiling { granted: g, ceiling: c } =>
      join(["the policy sized a grant of ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: g)), " bytes against a ceiling of ", to_string(finite_byte_size_count(size: c)), " bytes; the grant is REFUSED and is never reduced to the ceiling -- raise the cell row, lower the policy allowance or reduce the demand"], "")
  }
}

// THE SOLE MINT. The ceiling is decided FIRST: a grant the cell cannot hold is refused whatever the
// Work asked for, and a demand that exceeds the cell is above the ceiling AND below nothing, so
// reporting the ceiling sends the reader to the cell row, which is the remedy. The minimum is
// decided second, and only for a grant that was derived from one.
fn issue_memory_grant<P, A>(
  work: FabricIdentity<P, WorkKey>,
  attempt: FabricIdentity<P, ExecutionAttemptKey>,
  granted: ByteSize,
  ceiling: FiniteByteSize,
  provenance: MemoryGrantProvenance,
  policy: DeclarationRef,
  authority: A,
) -> MemoryGrantIssue<P, A> {
  if byte_size_count(b: granted) > finite_byte_size_count(size: ceiling) {
    MemoryGrantRefused { refusal: GrantAboveCeiling { granted: granted, ceiling: ceiling } }
  } else {
    match provenance {
      GrantedAgainstStatedRequirement { required: need } =>
        if byte_size_count(b: granted) < byte_size_count(b: need.min_bytes) {
          MemoryGrantRefused {
            refusal: GrantBelowStatedMinimum { required: need.min_bytes, granted: granted },
          }
        } else {
          MemoryGranted {
            grant: MemoryGrant {
              work: work, attempt: attempt, granted: granted,
              provenance: provenance, policy: policy, authority: authority,
            },
          }
        }
      GrantedUnderCompatibilityPermit { permit: _ } =>
        MemoryGranted {
          grant: MemoryGrant {
            work: work, attempt: attempt, granted: granted,
            provenance: provenance, policy: policy, authority: authority,
          },
        }
    }
  }
}

// A DEFAULTED GRANT MAY NOT RENDER AS "MEMORY REQUIREMENT CHECKED", AND THAT IS A CONSTRUCTION
// RATHER THAN A STYLE RULE FOR THE RENDERER.
//
// The two grants carry materially different evidence. One says "the Work stated N and the policy
// authorized G >= N" -- a checked relation between two facts. The other says "THE WORK STATED NO
// REQUIREMENT; fleet compatibility policy nevertheless granted G" -- no relation was checked at
// all, because there was nothing to check it against, and the number is an operator's bet that a
// legacy Work will fit. Rendering the second with the first's sentence is the conflation
// product.fabric.envelope already names for the matching fold: absence of observation reported as
// positive clearance.
//
// So the standing is a COPRODUCT derived from the provenance, with no arm that reports a check for
// a defaulted grant, and there is no route from a permitted grant to the checked arm. A renderer
// that wants the strong sentence must match the strong arm, and the compiler will not let it forget
// the other one exists. The weak arm names the permit's issuer so a reader can go argue with the
// right authority.
type MemoryGrantStanding
  = RequirementCheckedAtOrAbove { required: ByteSize, granted: ByteSize }
  | NoRequirementStatedCompatibilityDefault { permit: MemoryCompatibilityPermit, granted: ByteSize }

fn memory_grant_standing<P, A>(g: MemoryGrant<P, A>) -> MemoryGrantStanding {
  match g.provenance {
    GrantedAgainstStatedRequirement { required: r } =>
      RequirementCheckedAtOrAbove { required: r.min_bytes, granted: g.granted }
    GrantedUnderCompatibilityPermit { permit: p } =>
      NoRequirementStatedCompatibilityDefault { permit: p, granted: g.granted }
  }
}

fn memory_grant_standing_text(s: MemoryGrantStanding) -> String {
  match s {
    RequirementCheckedAtOrAbove { required: q, granted: g } =>
      join(["memory requirement checked: the Work states a minimum of ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: q)), " bytes and this attempt is authorized to consume ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: g)), " bytes"], "")
    NoRequirementStatedCompatibilityDefault { permit: p, granted: g } =>
      join(["the Work stated no memory requirement; fleet compatibility policy nevertheless granted ", to_string(byte_size_count(b: g)), " bytes, permitted by ", p.permitted_by.decl_name as String, " because ", p.reason as String, " -- no memory requirement was checked for this attempt"], "")
  }
}
